# Packages-Root.arch — the ARCH LINUX variant of Packages-Root (migrating from Artix, in
# progress: docs/ARCH-BASE.md). Same image, same allow-list rules; every name is an official
# Arch Linux package ([core]/[extra], checked against the Arch Linux Archive snapshot of
# 2026-11-25) or a [runink] package. NOTHING READS THIS FILE YET: the shipped ISO is built
# from Packages-Root with Artix packages until the Arch-based builder (ARCH-BASE.md §5, D2)
# reads this list and its ISO boots (D4), when this file replaces Packages-Root. A change
# to Packages-Root during the migration is made here too. [runink] names marked PROVISIONAL
# are packages the migration has not built yet.
#
# The allow-list for Runink River, the developer workstation (DEsk on
# Wayland, s6, encrypted ZFS root, one pinned kernel).
#
# The closure of this list is checked against forbidden.closure and the systemd policy in
# docs/ARCH-BASE.md §4 (river lint closure, once it reads the pinned Arch databases).
#
# NOT on this image, deliberately: a Kubernetes node, NAT64, platform runtimes, model
# payloads. A downstream distribution that needs them builds its own profile from this
# repository's packaging (docs/BUILD.md, "Downstream distributions").

# ── base + boot ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Arch's `base` meta-package hard-depends on systemd and systemd-sysvcompat, so its members
# are listed instead (Arch base 3-3 at the 2026-11-25 snapshot, minus those two).
filesystem
gcc-libs
glibc
bash
coreutils
file
findutils
gawk
grep
procps-ng
sed
tar
gettext
pciutils
psmisc
shadow
bzip2
gzip
xz
licenses
iputils
iproute2
# pacman: Arch's hard-depends on systemd (the alpm sandbox user via systemd-sysusers); the
# [runink] rebuild without it shadows it (ARCH-BASE.md §3 row 11, C4). PROVISIONAL.
pacman
archlinux-keyring
# udev and the standalone sysusers/tmpfiles tools, which Artix shipped as part of its base and
# Arch ships only inside systemd: built in [runink] from the systemd source at the snapshot's
# systemd-libs version (ARCH-BASE.md §3 rows 8-9, C4). PROVISIONAL names.
runink-udev
runink-sysusers-tmpfiles
linux-runink

# mkinitcpio: Arch's hard-depends on systemd; the [runink] rebuild shadows it (C4). PROVISIONAL.
mkinitcpio
grub
efibootmgr

# ── firmware ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A workstation fleet is not one SKU: the reference machine is AMD (Radeon, HawkPoint), and
# amdgpu will not initialise without its firmware split. Both vendors are listed so one image
# boots either.
linux-firmware-amdgpu
linux-firmware-intel
# NETWORK FIRMWARE FOR HARDWARE THAT IS NOT INTEL. Added 2026-10-18 after the
# owner booted the medium on real hardware and it recognised NO NIC and no
# wifi card. linux-firmware-intel carries iwlwifi, so an Intel machine was
# fine and the gap was invisible on the reference hardware; linux-firmware-
# amdgpu is a GPU split and carries no network firmware at all. Anything with
# a Realtek, Broadcom, MediaTek, Atheros or Qualcomm radio or NIC had nothing
# to load, so the kernel found no device and the installer's first screen --
# which is the network step -- had nothing to offer.
#
# These are the NETWORK splits only, not the whole linux-firmware. An
# INSTALLER medium has to come up on hardware nobody chose in advance, and a
# machine that cannot reach the network cannot be installed; a machine with an
# unaccelerated GPU still installs (the nomodeset entry exists for that). That
# asymmetry is why the GPU splits stay vendor-scoped and these do not.
#
# All six verified present in Arch [core] at the 2026-11-25 archive snapshot.
linux-firmware-realtek
linux-firmware-broadcom
linux-firmware-atheros
linux-firmware-mediatek
linux-firmware-qcom
linux-firmware-marvell
# CPU microcode, loaded early by GRUB on the installed machine and the live medium (Arch [core],
# checked against the 2026-11-25 snapshot). Requested by the Arch ISO builder (#108).
intel-ucode
amd-ucode

# ── ZFS root ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Encrypted ZFS root (invariant 3); the datasets are created with compression=zstd by the
# installer.
runink-zfs
runink-zfs-utils

# ── installer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# From the pinned [runink] local repo: river-hwprobe + river-plan (the
# install plan runink-install confirms by disk serial) and river-netsetup + river-netcheck
# (the network-first step the live session opens with).
runink-installer
# Live-medium boot and the installer's disk tools (the installed machine is a clone of the live
# rootfs, so they stay on it too).
squashfs-tools
# NOT mkinitcpio-nfs-utils (removed 2026-10-01). It ships `ipconfig` and `nfsmount` for
# mkinitcpio's `net` hook — NFS-root boot — and no initramfs this image builds names that
# hook: the live one comes from runink-live-initcpio (ARCH-BASE.md §3 row 14), the installed
# one from root-overlay/etc/mkinitcpio.conf.d/zfs.conf.
# Nothing in the resolved closure requires it, nothing in this repository referenced it, and
# docs/OWN-BASE.md already listed it as a drop.
# pacstrap, arch-chroot, genfstab (Artix's artools-base had basestrap, artix-chroot, fstabgen).
arch-install-scripts
dialog
parted
gptfdisk
dosfstools

# ── s6 init ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# s6, NOT systemd. This is a hard invariant of the image and it constrains the desktop:
# every service below must have an s6 counterpart, which is why the -s6 packages are named
# explicitly rather than relying on a desktop meta-package to drag in systemd units.
# The skarnet stack from base/recipes, built into [runink] (ARCH-BASE.md §3 row 5, C1).
# Arch carries none of it. PROVISIONAL: the names the s6 packaging settles on.
s6
s6-rc
s6-linux-init
# PID 1 glue and the base service tree (replaces Artix's s6-scripts), and the service
# definitions for every bundle the Artix *-s6 packages shipped: dbus, elogind,
# NetworkManager, sshd, chrony, bluetoothd, cupsd (ARCH-BASE.md §3 rows 6-7, C3). PROVISIONAL.
runink-s6-base
runink-s6-services
# chrony + chrony-s6: the clock. NOTHING on this image synchronised time before 2026-10-02,
# and an unsynchronised clock is not a cosmetic problem: TLS certificates are rejected as
# not-yet-valid or expired, k0s/Kubernetes client certificates and tokens fail the same way,
# and GitHub App JWTs are refused with "'exp' claim is too far in the future" — which is how
# the self-hosted runner on an image built from this profile had every private-repo CI job
# 401 after drifting ~39s ahead of GitHub. Its `chrony` s6-rc bundle is in runink-s6-services.
chrony

# ── network ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
networkmanager
wpa_supplicant
openssh
iptables
nftables

# ── desktop: DEsk on Wayland ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEsk (owner decision 2026-10-30; docs/DESKTOP-DESK-PLAN.md) replaced KDE Plasma: one
# wlroots compositor that is the whole desktop — windows as paper on a desk, the drawer, the
# bookcase, the post box (notifications), the tray and the front-door lock — built in
# [runink] from DEsk's release plus the Runink fork's patches. Plasma, KWin, SDDM, Breeze, Qt
# and KDE Frameworks left the image with it (forbidden.closure keeps them out).
runink-desk
runink-wlroots0.18
# The login screen: greetd, running gtkgreet fullscreen in cage, as an s6 service from
# runink-greetd-s6 (Arch has greetd and no s6 service). greetd itself is Arch [extra].
greetd
runink-greetd-s6
runink-cage
runink-gtkgreet
# sd-bus for DEsk's post box, tray and logind power (elogind's library, not systemd's). Arch has
# no elogind: built in [runink] from the pinned upstream release (ARCH-BASE.md §3 row 10, C4).
# PROVISIONAL name; it must provide libelogind for runink-wlroots0.18 and seatd.
runink-elogind
# What DEsk's session helper starts (its autostart entries) and what it opens. Each is a
# small GTK or C program; none is Python, which the image does not carry: so no udiskie
# (automounting and the low-battery letter are river-desk's, runink-installer), blueman
# (Bluetooth: runink-bluetuith, below), system-config-printer (CUPS's own pages on
# localhost:631) or ibus (152 MiB, Python setup).
mate-polkit
network-manager-applet
nm-connection-editor
# Bluetooth devices: bluetuith (Go, MIT) in a terminal, built in [runink]; DEsk's Bluetooth row
# opens it (river-bluetooth).
runink-bluetuith
gnome-keyring
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
xorg-xwayland
xdg-user-dirs
xdg-utils
wdisplays
# Print and Alt+Print (desk-screenshot): grim, the clipboard, and the letter saying where.
grim
wl-clipboard
libnotify
# rsvg-convert: DEsk rasterises the SVG icons of the programs on its shelves with it (without it
# their sheets show an initial). gtk3 already depends on librsvg, so this adds nothing to the
# closure; it is named so the desk keeps it if gtk3 stops needing it.
librsvg
# DEsk's own typefaces (URW base 35).
gsfonts
# NOT here (the v0.2.1 review, docs/DESKTOP-DESK-PLAN.md phase C; Arch depends lists of 2026-11-23):
# power-profiles-daemon (needs upower and polkit, a service s6 does not have yet; powerprofilesctl is
# Python) and iio-sensor-proxy (libssc, polkit, the same missing service) for DEsk's POWER SAVE
# and auto-rotate; yad (webkit2gtk) for the screen-share chooser; ibus (Python) and fcitx5
# (depends on systemd; DEsk's own fields type through IBus only) for CJK input.

# ── applications (daily desktop) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DEsk's standard set: the classic GTK programs, which leave their frame to the desk and lie
# on it as paper. foot is the terminal (Wayland-native, under 1 MiB).
foot
thunar
tumbler
gvfs
udisks2
mousepad
ristretto
atril
engrampa
parole

# Browser. Its telemetry, studies and default-browser nag are switched off system-wide by
# root-overlay/etc/firefox/policies/policies.json (Mozilla enterprise policies).
firefox

# ── boot splash: Plymouth (workstation ONLY) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The RIVER `river` script theme (root-overlay/usr/share/plymouth/themes/river). Arch
# ships plymouth in [extra] and its mkinitcpio hook has a non-systemd path (add_runscript;
# the busybox hook starts plymouthd), so it is s6-clean. Its dependencies (cairo, pango,
# fontconfig, freetype2, libx11, libdrm, libxkbcommon, xkeyboard-config, glib2) are
# already in this closure: measured with pactree (2026-09-22), plymouth adds only itself
# (measured on the Artix list; re-measure on this one).
# s6 has no plymouth-quit.service; root-overlay/etc/s6/adminsv/plymouth-quit does that job.
plymouth

# ── graphics: mesa ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# vulkan-radeon for the AMD reference machine, vulkan-intel so the same image is usable on
# Intel hardware. libva for hardware video decode.
mesa
vulkan-icd-loader
vulkan-radeon
vulkan-intel
# (Artix's libva-mesa-driver: Arch folded the VA-API driver into mesa.)
mesa-utils

# ── multimedia: codecs + hardware video ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# GStreamer (what Parole and Firefox's WebRTC use) plus ffmpeg. intel-media-driver is the
# Meteor Lake (iHD) VA-API driver; the AMD
# reference machine decodes through libva-mesa-driver above. libva-utils gives vainfo.
gst-plugins-base
gst-plugins-good
gst-plugins-bad
gst-plugins-ugly
gst-libav
ffmpeg
intel-media-driver
libva-utils

# ── audio ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
pipewire-alsa
wireplumber
# JACK clients through PipeWire (pro-audio apps without a separate jack2 server). The
# volume is DEsk's own (its Control Panel, through wpctl), so no mixer program is added.
pipewire-jack

# ── bluetooth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No Bluetooth applet: blueman, the one DEsk's Shell Settings opens, is Python. bluetoothctl
# (bluez-utils) pairs devices. Its s6 service is in runink-s6-services above.
bluez
bluez-utils

# ── printing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No printer program: system-config-printer is Python, so printers are set up on CUPS's own
# page (http://localhost:631) in Firefox.
cups

# ── fonts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ttf-dejavu
ttf-liberation
noto-fonts
noto-fonts-emoji
noto-fonts-cjk
# NOT noto-fonts-extra (removed 2026-10-01). 331.3 MiB for one package, and it adds no
# SCRIPT this image could not already render: measured against the Artix file database, all
# 84 font families in noto-fonts-extra are among the 226 in noto-fonts, and what it adds is
# extra WIDTHS and WEIGHTS of them (Condensed / SemiCondensed / ExtraCondensed, and a couple
# of Semibold faces). noto-fonts, noto-fonts-cjk and noto-fonts-emoji keep the coverage.
# Nothing in the resolved closure depends on it. What a user loses: those width and weight
# variants in a font picker.

# ── developer toolchain ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A developer workstation clones and builds: base-devel, git and curl are its tools (invariant
# 5 is written for this; see AGENTS.md).
base-devel
git
curl
rsync
vim
# The terminal greeting: fastfetch (the machine summary), printed by
# etc/profile.d/runink-greeting.sh. The login shell is bash, from `base`; fish was dropped
# 2026-10-02 (owner: "Do we really need the fish?") and nothing else in the closure needs it.
fastfetch
sudo
go
zstd

# ── compression + power ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# zram is configured by the s6 service in root-overlay (zram-generator is a systemd
# generator and cannot be used here). util-linux provides zramctl.
util-linux
cpupower

# ── sandbox ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# bubblewrap: unprivileged (not setuid) and adds only itself to the closure. river-sandbox
# (root-overlay/usr/local/bin) runs untrusted code under it (invariant 8).
bubblewrap
